WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.030 --> 00:00:03.080
Hi, I'm Tessa Rodes and I'm a designer on IBM Cloud

00:00:03.080 --> 00:00:07.780
and today we're going to talk about Platform as a Service (PaaS),

00:00:07.780 --> 00:00:11.780
and it's hard to define Platform as a Service without talking about its neighbors,

00:00:11.820 --> 00:00:14.660
Infrastructure as a Service (IaaS),

00:00:14.700 --> 00:00:17.580
and Software as a Service (SaaS).

00:00:17.580 --> 00:00:20.100
So, let's start with the foundation here with IaaS.

00:00:20.100 --> 00:00:22.800
IaaS is a set of compute, networking,

00:00:22.800 --> 00:00:25.700
and storage resources that have been virtualized by a vendor

00:00:25.700 --> 00:00:29.280
so that a user can access and configure
them any way they want.

00:00:29.280 --> 00:00:33.720
In design we have a concept of talking about users called personas,

00:00:33.720 --> 00:00:39.520
and the persona for IaaS is a System Admin,

00:00:39.520 --> 00:00:43.040
or an IT Admin.

00:00:43.040 --> 00:00:46.900
Let's jump up to the top with Software as a Service, that's the easy one.

00:00:46.900 --> 00:00:48.700
Software as a Service is just software

00:00:48.700 --> 00:00:50.220
that you don't have to install on your machine

00:00:50.220 --> 00:00:52.040
and you don't have to manually update

00:00:52.040 --> 00:00:57.040
and so the user for Software as a Service
could be anyone.

00:00:57.040 --> 00:00:59.220
In fact, if you're watching this on YouTube right now

00:00:59.220 --> 00:01:01.360
then you're a user of Software as a Service.

00:01:01.360 --> 00:01:05.750
It's usually charged on a subscription
model rather than a one-time license fee.

00:01:05.750 --> 00:01:08.659
And that brings us to Platform
as a Service,

00:01:08.659 --> 00:01:12.840
PaaS takes advantage of all the
virtualized resources from IaaS and

00:01:12.840 --> 00:01:15.619
then just obstructs them away so the
user doesn't have to worry about

00:01:15.619 --> 00:01:22.710
managing any of those virtualized
resources. The user for PaaS is not a

00:01:22.710 --> 00:01:33.180
System Admin usually, it's usually a Dev.
In IBM we call this Dev "Jane", that's the

00:01:33.180 --> 00:01:37.320
name of our persona. And so this whole
metaphor, the pyramid metaphor, is meant

00:01:37.320 --> 00:01:42.689
to indicate that as you move down you're
increasing complexity in terms of your

00:01:42.689 --> 00:01:46.049
knowledge and and management of
infrastructure resources and you're

00:01:46.049 --> 00:01:53.460
increasing the ease of use. Another
metaphor I use when I'm talking to the

00:01:53.460 --> 00:01:57.180
designers on my team about PaaS is
having to do with a car.

00:01:57.180 --> 00:02:05.880
So, in this metaphor IaaS is like leasing
a car. So, if you've got leased a car

00:02:05.880 --> 00:02:10.590
you probably did a lot of research and
you care about the specs of the car and

00:02:10.590 --> 00:02:13.330
the performance,
you care about the color of the car, what

00:02:13.330 --> 00:02:19.450
kind of car it is. You're the one driving and
you're paying for it. You're also paying

00:02:19.450 --> 00:02:25.150
for the gas and any tolls or maintenance.
With Platform as a Service

00:02:25.150 --> 00:02:30.550
in this metaphor that's more like
renting a car. So, say you're on vacation

00:02:30.550 --> 00:02:34.570
and you just got out off the Airport and
you're going to pick up your rental car,

00:02:34.570 --> 00:02:39.550
you don't really care what color it is,
you don't even care about the specs

00:02:39.550 --> 00:02:43.090
of it, but you're still driving and
you're paying for the gas and any tolls

00:02:43.090 --> 00:02:48.880
you go through. Software as a Service is
again the easiest one. That one's more

00:02:48.880 --> 00:02:53.800
like getting a taxi, or an Uber. So, with the
taxi or Uber you don't care at all

00:02:53.800 --> 00:02:57.490
about what kind of car it is, what color
it is, and in fact you're not even the

00:02:57.490 --> 00:03:01.450
one driving or paying for gas or any
tolls because that's baked into the

00:03:01.450 --> 00:03:06.010
price. So, let's think about what that
means in terms of cloud computing and

00:03:06.010 --> 00:03:12.010
what the vendor provides and what the
developer, or "Jane", provides. So, the

00:03:12.010 --> 00:03:25.690
vendor provides servers, storage,
networking, virtualization, middleware, the

00:03:25.690 --> 00:03:32.650
operating system, and the runtime. All
that leaves for the developer to provide

00:03:32.650 --> 00:03:43.590
is their data and applications. So, this
is and this is the vendor, cloud vendor.

00:03:43.590 --> 00:03:48.209
So, there are some pros and cons with
that just like with leasing, renting, or

00:03:48.209 --> 00:03:54.870
getting a taxi. The pros for using
Platform as a Service

00:03:54.870 --> 00:04:02.380
are generally assumed that it's
quite fast and easy to get an app up and

00:04:02.380 --> 00:04:09.190
running which is great. It's easy to
create and delete the resources which is

00:04:09.190 --> 00:04:13.780
important for say you're running a
proof-of-concept app for a conference

00:04:13.780 --> 00:04:17.320
and you want to spin it up right before
the conference and then delete it right

00:04:17.320 --> 00:04:22.479
after, you're not paying for the whole
month and it's easy to spin it up and

00:04:22.479 --> 00:04:24.790
delete it.
Also,

00:04:24.790 --> 00:04:30.810
that really results in some cost
benefits. Another cost benefit of

00:04:30.810 --> 00:04:34.780
Platform as a Service is that you don't
have to pay for a full time system or

00:04:34.780 --> 00:04:40.479
IT admin to run this app, they can
focus on something else. And another Pro

00:04:40.479 --> 00:04:45.550
is that a lot of providers of
Platform as a Service have been building

00:04:45.550 --> 00:04:50.740
out some tools on their platform. So, you
can use DevOps tools, collaboration tools,

00:04:50.740 --> 00:04:57.400
and there's a lot of API marketplaces
that have been coming out which is great

00:04:57.400 --> 00:05:00.910
because you can just take those services
and plug them into your app, again still

00:05:00.910 --> 00:05:08.710
without having to worry about any of
this. There are some Cons to using

00:05:08.710 --> 00:05:14.530
Platform as a Service. This big one is
lack of control but some people will

00:05:14.530 --> 00:05:20.229
still say that that is a Pro because
although some people like to pull all

00:05:20.229 --> 00:05:23.260
the levers and push all the buttons
having to do with fine-tuning their

00:05:23.260 --> 00:05:26.830
infrastructure, a lot of people don't
want to have to worry about that, nor do

00:05:26.830 --> 00:05:35.440
they have the understanding or ability
to do that. Another Con is the vendor

00:05:35.440 --> 00:05:42.729
lock-in, it's a little bit hard to
migrate your app unless you're using an

00:05:42.729 --> 00:05:46.260
open-source framework like Cloud Foundry,
it's a little hard to migrate

00:05:46.260 --> 00:05:50.320
Platform as a Service app from one cloud
to another. So make sure you trust and

00:05:50.320 --> 00:05:56.260
like your cloud provider before you
choose it. And then finally the

00:05:56.260 --> 00:06:01.710
performance at scale can be an issue.

00:06:01.740 --> 00:06:07.060
Performance at scale, so say Jane the
developer had a Platform as a Service

00:06:07.060 --> 00:06:10.090
proof of concept app from that
conference, and then she released it to

00:06:10.090 --> 00:06:14.289
10,000 people the next day. It definitely
won't have the same in and out

00:06:14.289 --> 00:06:18.400
performance as if it was running on a
dedicated bare metal server for example.

00:06:18.400 --> 00:06:22.900
So, that's a consideration to have. So, I
hope that helps you understand the

00:06:22.900 --> 00:06:26.110
benefits and the reasons for choosing
Platform as a Service. Thanks for

00:06:26.110 --> 00:06:29.710
watching. If you have questions please
drop us a line below. If you want to see

00:06:29.710 --> 00:06:32.560
more videos like this in the future 
please like and subscribe and don't

00:06:32.560 --> 00:06:35.890
forget you can always get started on
cloud at no cost by signing up for a

00:06:35.890 --> 00:06:39.010
free
IBM Cloud account.

